WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.660
Annex A in ISO/IEC 42001 is useful because it gives organizations a reference set of AI-specific control themes.

2
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:18.200
It should not be reproduced as a table in training, and it should not be treated as a universal list that every system implements identically.

3
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:28.380
The implementation task is to translate relevant control ideas into behavior that fits the organization's risks, obligations, systems, and lifecycle.

4
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.960
That translation is where governance becomes practical rather than ceremonial.

5
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.200
One theme is policy and internal organization.

6
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:45.020
Direction for developing, providing, acquiring, and using AI has to be owned, communicated, and reflected in procedures.

7
00:00:45.120 --> 00:01:00.240
Responsibilities, authorities, segregation of duties, escalation paths, and cross-functional coordination should be clear enough that real decisions do not stall between product, data, legal, risk, procurement, and engineering teams.

8
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:05.540
A control that nobody can execute during a release cycle is not yet operational.

9
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.740
Another theme is resources.

10
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:18.240
AI systems rely on data, models, tools, infrastructure, computing capacity, documentation, human expertise, and supporting services.

11
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:26.020
Governance should know which resources matter, who controls them, how they change, and what evidence shows they are suitable.

12
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:34.760
A model cannot be governed well if the organization cannot explain the data pipeline, vendor dependency, monitoring tool, or human review role that supports it.

13
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:46.480
Controls also need to cover impact assessment, lifecycle practices, data governance, information for interested parties, responsible use, and third-party relationships.

14
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:58.660
Data governance includes sources, provenance, rights, permissions, quality, relevance, preparation, protection, retention, and traceability.

15
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:06.760
Information for interested parties may help people understand, use, oversee, challenge, or respond to an AI system.

16
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:14.140
Supplier governance should define responsibilities without pretending the organization has transferred all accountability to a vendor.

17
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.960
Customer responsibilities may also matter when the organization provides AI to others.

18
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:30.840
A practical control design pattern identifies the control objective, owner, procedure, resources, evidence, measurement, exception handling, and review frequency.

19
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:47.660
Evidence may include approved policies, decision records, inventory entries, assessments, test results, monitoring reports, supplier reviews, competence records, change approvals, incident records, and corrective actions.

20
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.120
Documents alone do not prove effectiveness.

21
00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:58.260
Interviews, samples, system records, observations, and operating results often show whether controls work.

22
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.480
The best control evidence links what the organization said it would do with what actually happened.

23
00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:13.660
A control library can be useful, but only when it is connected to procedures, people, records, sampled operation, and decisions that improve AI governance.

24
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.640
Otherwise it is only a catalog, not evidence of controlled behavior.

25
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.740
The evidence should survive routine sampling.
